item.description:
{
  "version": "1.28.0",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Fork Changes",
      "items": [
        { "title": "Banners autolink URLs and file paths", "text": "A bare `https://` URL or a `/`, `~/`, `./`, or `../` path in a pane banner is now clickable on its own — no `[text](url)` needed. Clicking a file link reveals it in Finder; Cmd-click opens it in a viewer, and the right-click menu adds Open in Side Pane and Copy Path. Dot-relative paths resolve against the pane's working directory." },
        { "title": "Banner links look and behave like links", "text": "Links show a dotted underline at rest that goes solid on hover, and a right-click menu offers Side Pane / New Window / Default Browser / Copy Link. Clicking a link no longer collapses the banner out from under you." },
        { "title": "Viewer panes get their own keyboard shortcuts", "text": "Cmd-R reloads the focused viewer in place and Cmd-D drops the caret into its address field with the address selected. Both apply only while a viewer holds focus, so they keep doing their usual thing in a terminal pane. Escape now cancels an address edit and hands focus back to the page." },
        { "title": "`+send-keys` actually submits", "text": "`ghoztty +send-keys --target=pane \"some message\" Enter` now sends the message instead of leaving it parked unsent in the composer. Text is framed as a bracketed paste so the trailing Enter reads as a keypress rather than a newline inside pasted text." },
        { "title": "What's New in Ghoztty…", "text": "A new menu window with Client and Agent tabs shows what changed in each, and the update chip's pre-update dialog now previews the incoming version's notes before you commit." }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "version": "1.29.0",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Fork Changes",
      "items": [
        { "title": "Git diffs in a viewer pane", "text": "`ghoztty +split --view=git-status:` opens a real diff review pane — a native changed-files tree with a filter on the left, red/green syntax-highlighted hunks on the right, and next/previous-change plus unified⇄side-by-side controls in the nav bar. Point it at a range (`git-diff:main...HEAD`), a single commit (`git-diff:a1b2c3d`), or just `git-diff:` for \"this branch vs. main\". A `git-status:` pane re-checks the working tree every couple of seconds, so edits and `git add`s show up without a reload. Everything a viewer already does works here — `+reload`, the address bar, back/forward, and session restore, which re-runs the diff rather than restoring a stale snapshot." },
        { "title": "See what your sessions are actually doing", "text": "The machine chooser now shows a live per-session CPU meter, including for machines reached over the relay, and the Activity Monitor reports true per-core %CPU and tells you which pane owns each process. Both are fed by a pushed stream rather than polling, so the roster updates the moment something changes — window renames included — instead of on a timer." },
        { "title": "The New Window picker stays out of your way", "text": "It's modeless now, so you can keep working with it open, and its \"See Activity\" button covers This Mac too. The session list stops offering to resume sessions whose pane you just closed, and no longer freezes while the dialog is open." },
        { "title": "Links leave Ghoztty when they should", "text": "A link that opens a new surface (`target=\"_blank\"`, `window.open()`) now goes to your default browser, where your cookies and logins actually live, instead of a logged-out viewer pane. Cmd-click still keeps the popup in Ghoztty." },
        { "title": "A window waiting on you reads as \"(question)\"", "text": "The activity-state suffix in the title shows a human label instead of the raw `needs_input` token. The machine tokens behind `+set-state`, OSC 7777, and the accessibility attribute are unchanged, so existing hooks and tools keep working." },
        { "title": "Auto-named windows are targetable right away", "text": "A window opened with Cmd-N gets a name like `window-7`, and `+set-state`/`+send-keys`/`+close --target=window-7` now find it without something having run `+list` first. Previously those calls failed silently for anything that discards stderr." },
        { "title": "Help and About point at Ghoztty", "text": "The version link opens this fork's release page instead of upstream Ghostty's docs, and the Help menu links follow suit. Thanks to @ZekeAranyLucas for the Help/About fix." }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "version": "1.30.0",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Fork Changes",
      "items": [
        { "title": "HTML files open as real pages", "text": "`ghoztty +split --view=mock/index.html` now renders the page instead of showing you its source. Its own CSS, JavaScript, images, and fonts run, links follow in the pane, and Back/Forward work across it — so a scaffolded mock or prototype is viewable straight from disk, with no `python3 -m http.server` standing in front of it. Saving the file re-renders it in place and keeps your scroll position, so you're not thrown back to the top of a long page. Assets are read from the page's own directory, so keep shared files under it; an edited sibling stylesheet needs `+reload`/Cmd+R to get past WebKit's cache." },
        { "title": "The fork runs CI again", "text": "Pull requests build and test for real, with the Zig and Swift halves reported as separate steps so a failure points at the right half. Its first real run caught a remote-handshake bug plus two flaky viewer tests, all fixed in this release." }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "version": "1.31.0",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Agent integrations",
      "items": [
        { "title": "Copilot CLI is a first-class agent runtime", "text": "Ghoztty now sets itself up with GitHub Copilot CLI the same way it does with Claude Code, so a Copilot pane gets Ghoztty's banner automation and session-start wiring too. The setup path was rebuilt around a runtime abstraction, so adding the next agent is a spec file rather than a fork of the whole flow." },
        { "title": "Skills and hooks now ship inside Ghoztty", "text": "The skills that teach your agent how to drive panes, splits, viewers, and banners are bundled with the app instead of arriving as a separately-versioned Claude plugin. A skill can no longer describe a CLI flag your installed Ghoztty doesn't have." },
        { "title": "An Agent Integrations panel", "text": "See at a glance which runtimes are set up, which are out of date, and which aren't installed — and set up, update, or cleanly uninstall each one. Installs are atomic and roll back on failure, and Ghoztty only ever touches files it wrote itself." },
        { "title": "The Claude plugin hands over automatically", "text": "If you had the standalone Ghoztty Claude plugin, the first launch after updating asks once whether to retire it. Say yes and Ghoztty uninstalls it through Claude's own CLI, carries your banner state across, and cleans up the leftover symlink. Either answer retires the prompt for good." },
        { "title": "Panes stay busy while background work is still running", "text": "The activity state a pane reports is now Ghoztty's own, and it understands background subagents. A pane no longer flips to idle the moment the main loop goes quiet while its subagents are still working, and a killed or cancelled agent can't strand a pane on *busy* forever." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Command line",
      "items": [
        { "title": "`+send-keys` submits when you meant to submit", "text": "`--target=t \"prompt\\n\"` now presses Enter, and so does `--enter`. Interior newlines still paste as content, so a multi-line prompt arrives intact and submits once. Any misspelled flag is now a loud error naming the three spellings that work, instead of being silently typed into your pane." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Machine chooser",
      "items": [
        { "title": "A taller, sortable session list", "text": "The machine's identity moved up into the header band, giving the list back about 50pt of rows. **CPU** and **Name** are clickable column headers — click to sort, click again to flip — and your choice sticks. Rows sort by the percentage you can actually see, so idle machines stop reshuffling under the cursor." },
        { "title": "Cmd-W over the chooser closes the chooser", "text": "It used to sail past the dialog and close a pane in the terminal window behind it, one you weren't even looking at." }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "version": "1.32.0",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Links that jump to a terminal",
      "items": [
        { "title": "A `ghoztty://` link can raise a window or pane", "text": "Write `ghoztty://focus/<name>` anywhere a link can live and clicking it brings that Ghoztty window or pane to the front. `<name>` is anything `--target` already accepts: a window name, an auto `window-3`, a pane name, or a pane id (`$GHOZTTY_PANE_ID`). A generated dashboard, a build report, or a status page can finally say \"jump to that terminal\" and have it work." },
        { "title": "It works everywhere a link renders", "text": "From your browser, from a page or rendered markdown in a viewer pane, and from a pane banner. Banner focus links get their own right-click menu (Focus in Ghoztty / Copy Link) instead of the side-pane and new-window options, which don't mean anything for a link that names a window rather than content." },
        { "title": "Focus is the only thing a link can do, on purpose", "text": "Any web page you visit can fire a registered URL scheme with no prompt and no way to tell who asked. A link that could open a shell or type into a pane would be remote code execution behind an `<a href>`, so raising an existing window is the whole feature. Nothing is ever created: a link naming a window that's gone tells you so instead of quietly focusing something else, and a burst of links produces one message rather than one per link." },
        { "title": "Debug builds keep their links to themselves", "text": "The debug build registers `ghoztty-debug://`, so macOS can't decide to route your links into a development build. Links clicked inside Ghoztty are handled in-process, so a document that hardcodes `ghoztty://` still lands in whichever build is rendering it." }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "version": "1.33.0",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Viewer panes",
      "items": [
        { "title": "Links that lead off-site now open in your real browser", "text": "Clicking a link out to another site from a live page (a dev server, a local HTML mock) used to follow it inside the pane, where Ghoztty's own cookie jar meant anything behind a login rendered logged-out. Cross-origin links now hand off to your default browser, so signing in works. Clicking around inside the same dev server or through a mock's own pages is untouched — that's what live pages are for." },
        { "title": "Every link gets Ghoztty's right-click menu", "text": "On any page, including sites we don't control, right-clicking a link offers the same actions a banner link does: open in a side pane, open in a new window, copy the link or path. The Cmd / Cmd-Shift modifiers work there too. Your text selection is respected — right-clicking inside a selection still gives you Copy and Look Up." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Command line",
      "items": [
        { "title": "`+rearrange` can place viewer panes", "text": "A layout that named a viewer pane failed with \"pane is no longer alive,\" about a pane `+list` had just reported, which left any window holding a preview pane permanently un-rearrangeable. Viewer panes now rearrange like any other, keep their content and scroll position, and focus lands where the layout asked." }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "version": "1.34.0",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Split panes",
      "items": [
        { "title": "Dragging a divider moves only that edge", "text": "With three or more panes in a row, dragging the left divider dragged the right one along with it, because each split stored a fraction and setting one rescaled the whole subtree. A divider drag is now measured in points against a snapshot of the layout taken at mouse-down, so only the two panes flanking that divider change and every other edge stays exactly where you put it. Push a pane to its minimum and the one behind it starts giving instead — and dragging past the minimum and back restores the squashed panes precisely. Keyboard resize follows the same math." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Viewer panes",
      "items": [
        { "title": "A website or HTML mock keeps its address bar on screen", "text": "A live page is something you navigate, so its address field and back/forward controls now stay visible instead of peeking in on hover. Markdown and code viewers are reading surfaces and keep the hover peek. The bar follows the pane's current content, so browsing a document out to the web pins it and going Back hands it away again." },
        { "title": "A squeezed viewer stays inside its pane", "text": "Narrowing a viewer past the width of its navigation buttons spilled the whole viewer out both sides — over the pane next door and off the window edge. A viewer is a leaf in the split tree like a terminal now: the tree decides the width and the content compresses to fit." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Command line",
      "items": [
        { "title": "A target-less `+split` lands in the pane that asked for it", "text": "`ghoztty +split` with no `--target` used whichever window was focused at the moment the app got around to handling it — so an agent in one pane asking for a side pane, plus a click somewhere else in the meantime, put the pane in the wrong window. It now anchors at the pane the command was actually run from. `--target`, `--pane`, and `--from-focused` still win, and a command run outside Ghoztty behaves as before." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Build",
      "items": [
        { "title": "`ghostty-vt` compiles again", "text": "The C formatter binding had fallen out of sync with its Zig counterpart, which stopped the library building. The missing option is mirrored across." }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "version": "1.35.0",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Viewer panes",
      "items": [
        { "title": "Cmd-F finds text in any viewer", "text": "A viewer pane had no way to search what it was showing. Cmd-F now opens a find bar in every mode — markdown, code, a local HTML page, a website, and a git diff — with the same behavior everywhere. Typing highlights every match with a live `3/17` count; Enter, Shift-Enter, Cmd-G and Cmd-Shift-G step through them and wrap. Escape closes the bar and clears the highlights but keeps your query, so Cmd-G picks it back up. It is honest about its limits too: a page with a frame says so, and a diff pane names the file it is searching." },
        { "title": "Images get a real image viewer", "text": "Opening an image in a viewer pane used to render its bytes as syntax-highlighted source. It now gets a proper image pane with trackpad pinch anchored where your fingers are, two-finger pan with elastic edges and momentum, and double-click to toggle between best-fit and 100%. 100% means one image pixel per device pixel, so a screenshot shows exactly what was captured, and best-fit never upscales, so a small icon stays crisp at its real size." },
        { "title": "The navigation bar stays put", "text": "Markdown and code panes were the last ones still hiding their nav bar until you reached for it — and that was the one mode where the content reflowed under your pointer and the only way out of the pane had to be hunted for with the mouse. The address field, Back, Forward and Home are now pinned open in every viewer mode." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Hero mode",
      "items": [
        { "title": "Scrolling a web page is smooth again", "text": "Scrolling a page in a hero-mode viewer was visibly janky: each carousel thumbnail cost about 30ms of web-process render time and fired every 150ms, so roughly a fifth of the frame budget went to a picture the size of a postage stamp. Thumbnails now pause while you are interacting with a pane — scrolling, typing, dragging a divider — and refresh once things settle. Measured over a 6-second continuous scroll, dropped frames went from 10.8% to zero, matching a build that takes no thumbnails at all. Thumbnails are also captured at the size they are actually displayed, and a wedged page can no longer freeze its thumbnail for the life of the pane." },
        { "title": "The divider is grabbable across its whole band", "text": "The hero divider showed a resize cursor over a 6pt band but could only be grabbed on the 1pt line drawn in the middle, so the pointer promised a target that wasn't there. It now has the same real grab zone as the regular split divider, reaching about 4pt into each pane." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Remote machines",
      "items": [
        { "title": "Disconnect from a remote window without ending the session", "text": "Closing a remote window always terminated the remote process, so there was no way to walk away from a session and come back to it. The close confirmation for a remote window now offers a third button — Disconnect — which leaves the remote process running and the session resumable from the machine chooser (Cmd-Shift-N). Remote windows are also confirmed even when idle now: ending a process on another machine isn't the recoverable thing an idle local shell is. Local windows, viewer panes, and `ghoztty +close` are unchanged." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Account and security",
      "items": [
        { "title": "Signing out actually removes this machine from your account", "text": "Signing out of the Ghoztty app on a relay-enrolled machine ended your session but left the machine enrolled — from any other client on the account it stayed listed, stayed online, and a session already bridged to it kept streaming. Sign-out is now a hard revocation of the machine the app is running on: the device row is deleted, every live connection is severed, and the local credential is destroyed. If the relay can't be reached you stay signed in rather than being told you're secure when you aren't; you can sign out anyway, and the revocation is retried at every launch and whenever the network comes back until the relay confirms it. Signing back in with the same account re-enrolls the machine under the name it had." }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
